Next comes specific backend guides like CANN (for Ascend NPU) which includes OS requirements, hardware specs like 310P notes about data types supported; Docker setup steps; Linux environment setup including driver installation; environment variables configurations related to memory pools.

Then under SYCL backend details: background info on SYCL from Intel oneAPI ecosystem enabling heterogeneous computing across CPUs/GPUs/FPGAs along with performance references on Intel GPUs using SYCL. It has recommended releases from February 2025 onwards detailing features added in each version such as Flash Attention support starting April-May 2024 or removal of NVIDIA/AMD GPU support due to missing plugins later in same year etc., plus Windows/Linux build scripts and configuration options via env vars during runtime.
  
There are also sections covering OpenVINO integration allowing inference acceleration on Intel hardware via their open source toolkit - highlighting supported devices ranging from CPUs all the way through NPUs while noting some limitations around model precision and operation coverage currently being worked upon as part of ongoing development efforts.
  
Other backends include AMD ZenDNN for EPYC processors focusing primarily on matrix multiplications accelerated through its LowOHA MatMul operator alongside instructions for setting up required dependencies both automatically via CMake or manually building ZenDNN themselves before linking it into llama.cpp project structure during cmake phase.

The Snapdragon-based devices section covers cross-compilation workflows using provided toolchains, how different execution modes (single device vs multi-device splits) work along with environment variable controls affecting NPU allocation strategies including things like VTCM memory management specifics related to handling large models exceeding typical session address space limits by dynamically mapping/unmapping buffers at runtime depending upon active batch processing needs.

Each backend document contains important details about prerequisites needed before compilation, specific CMake flags that might be necessary when enabling/disabling certain features based on target platform capabilities (e.g., GGML_CANN_MEM_POOL settings), known issues users should be aware of such as limitations in current operation support or performance tradeoffs between different quantization formats across various hardware targets etc., plus guidance about contributing patches back upstream by appropriately tagging GitHub issues/PRs with relevant prefixes like [CANN], [SYCL] etc. to ensure they get addressed promptly by respective maintainers teams.

