## CANN Backend for Ascend NPU
- OS support includes Linux, Windows
- Hardware requires specific Ascend devices like 310P
- Model precision support includes Q8_0, Q4_0 etc.
- Docker setup instructions available

## SYCL Backend for Intel GPUs
- Background on SYCL and oneAPI ecosystem 
- Recommended releases from Feb25 onwards detailing features added in each version (Flash Attention support started Apr-May '24)
- Removed NVIDIA/AMD GPU support due to plugin issues later in same year  
  
## OpenVINO Integration   
Allows inference acceleration on Intel hardware via their open source toolkit - highlights supported devices ranging from CPUs through NPUs while noting some limitations around model precision and operation coverage currently being worked upon as part of ongoing development efforts.

## AMD ZenDNN Support    
For EPYC processors focusing primarily on matrix multiplications accelerated through its LowOHA MatMul operator alongside instructions for setting up required dependencies both automatically via CMake or manually building ZenDNN themselves before linking it into llama.cpp project structure during cmake phase.

Snapdragon-based Devices:
Cross-compilation workflows using provided toolchains including different execution modes (single device vs multi-device splits) along with environment variable controls affecting NPU allocation strategies including VTCM memory management specifics related to handling large models exceeding typical session address space limits by dynamically mapping/unmapping buffers at runtime depending upon active batch processing needs. 

Each backend document contains important details about prerequisites needed before compilation, specific CMake flags that might be necessary when enabling/disabling certain features based on target platform capabilities plus guidance about contributing patches back upstream by appropriately tagging GitHub issues/PRs with relevant prefixes like [CANN], [SYCL] etc.
